{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And he offered sacrifices to the Elohim of his father Yitzhak.</b> It would have been fitting for Scripture to say, 'And he offered sacrifices to YHWH'; since it did not say so but said 'to the Elohim of his father,' the meaning is that he offered his sacrifices to the Pahad of his father Yitzhak, which is the attribute of Gevurah (Severity) closest to him, for he had already attained his portion therein, and on this account he dedicated the offering to the Elohim of his father Yitzhak. To this the masters of truth intended in their hidden language when they said in Bereshit Rabbah: a person is obligated in the honor of his father more than in the honor of his elder; he called his father 'the Pahad of Yitzhak,' and the attribute of Hesed (Lovingkindness) he called 'his elder.' And this same intention is hinted there in different language, namely what they said: at first one inquires after the well-being of the disciple and afterward after the well-being of the master — that is to say, that a person encounters the disciples first and the master after them, and therefore one inquires after their well-being first, and afterward after the master, who is last to be encountered and innermost relative to the disciples; and because Yitzhak was a disciple to Avraham, therefore Yaakov sacrificed to the Elohim of his father Yitzhak. And even so, it would have been fitting for Yaakov to give precedence to Yitzhak, either because he is his father or because he is a disciple to Avraham — and this is clear. And the offerings he brought were shelamim (peace-offerings), for because he saw the exile now beginning in his seed he was afraid of this, and therefore needed to bring this offering to the Pahad of Yitzhak; and they were shelamim in order to complete toward Him the attributes, and once he had offered his sacrifices toward this intention, then the Pahad of Yitzhak appeared to him in a relaxed attribute of judgment, to make clear that even though his sons would be exiled there, they would not be consumed in their exile. And therefore it says, ״Elohim״ in night visions — that is, the attribute of Gevurah and in a relaxed attribute of judgment, and this is what He promised him in glad tidings of redemption after the servitude and affliction."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:20:40.163670619Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}